  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Agricultural Technology Transfer Project in Response to the Climate Crisis for the Saemaul Plus End Poverty Program (SPEPP) in Bangladesh</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bangladesh is in the midst of transitioning from an agriculture -oriented industrial structure to industry-oriented structure. While agriculture still employs about 40% of the labor force, its share of GDP is declining to 11.3% (as of 2023). Despite abundant water resources and fertile soils, the country is highly vulnerable to natural disasters such as floods, droughts, and cyclones, and is far from self-sufficient. 
   This project of agricultural cooperation aims to eradicate poverty and enhance self-reliance in rural areas of two Bangladesh states by utilizing Korea&apos;s experience in developing rural communities during the Saemaul Movement.
   The Rural Development Administration seeks to increase agricultural productivity and secure sustainable income sources by supporting infrastructures such as agricultural training centers (ATC) and rural extension cooperatives for selection of climate-adaptative varieties, development and distribution of agricultural technologies, capacity building for public officials and farm workers on climate smart agriculture, etc., in response to climate change and to minimize damage.</narrative>
  </description>
